  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nJust a perfect early fall day out there today. Diurnal cumulus \nwill dissipate after sunset and I expect mostly clear skies at \nleast during the first half of the overnight. A backdoor cold \nfront will be sagging towards the region late this evening, but \nI do not think that significant prevents radiational cooling \nearly. As such I expect some valley fog to develop in the usual \nlocations prior to the clouds invading the area. Showers will \nbecome more likely just before sunrise mainly across the \nnorthern half of NH and the western Maine mtns into the Kennebec\nRiver Valley. NBM PoP is pretty low given the CAM depiction of \nprecip and QPF, so I leaned on the HREF for timing and location \nand brought the values up to 30 or 40 percent.\n\nBriefly high pressure will drift across the region Tue afternoon\nthru the overnight. That should allow for good radiational\ncooling conditions again, despite return flow starting towards\ndaybreak Wed.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure slides east Wednesday as low pressure moves \nslowly east toward James Bay and sends a cold front toward New \nEngland. Increasing southerly flow will advect higher moisture \ninto the region, potentially enough for some light showers \nacross the north, but mainly it will begin to feel more humid \nout as dewpoints are forecast to climb into the upper 50s to \nlower 60s with skies becoming mostly cloudy. Temperatures may be\nlimited to the 60s for northern areas due to more clouds while \nsouthern areas still reach the 70s. With the slower approach of \nthe front, prospects for thunder have lowered.\n\nThe better chances for showers arrive Wednesday evening and \nWednesday night as the front enters the region and better \nsupport moves in aloft. PWATs increase to 1.70\" to 2.00\" which \nis well above the 90th percentile, meaning rain rates could be \nbriefly heavy at times. But with the fast-moving nature of the \nsystem and drier antecedent conditions, not expecting any hydro \nissues. Models continue to favor the northern half of NH \nextending into the western ME mountains/foothills and Midcoast \nfor the higher chances/more QPF with ensemble means in pretty \ngood agreement of 0.25-0.50\" although there are some \nprobabilities exceeding 0.50\". Chances/amounts decrease with \nsouthern extent, and there are hints that southern NH sees less \n0.10\", which is certainly not ideal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n \nAny lingering showers will come to an end Thursday morning with\nmuch drier air moving in behind the initial front. West winds \nwill also ramp up in the morning and afternoon as mixing brings \ndown the higher winds from aloft and the surface low moves east \ntoward the Maritimes. Based on forecast soundings, gusts of \n25-35 mph appear likely, but there could be a few exceed 40 mph,\nper the top of the mixed layer. The cooler air doesn't move in \nuntil later in the day when a secondary front crosses, so it \nwill be a pretty warm day with highs expected to be upper 70s to\nlower 80s south of the mountains. \n\nTemperatures cool back down closer to normal for Friday and \nSaturday with highs mostly in the 70s and plenty of dry air from\nhigh pressure building into New England to keep skies mostly \nsunny with low humidity. Friday night may bring excellent \nradiational cooling to bring widespread lows in the 40s and some\n30s across northern valleys. \n\nThe high shifts to the east by Sunday as another low pressure \nmoves across the Hudson/James Bay region with its associated \ncold front approaching New England. This will bring another \nchance of showers either Sunday and/or Monday, which is \nsupported by the 20-30% NBM PoPs in this time window.",
